["er.eventlog.import-anchor/1",{"authority":{"logical_scope":"aep","stream_identity":"01a0cae6-7e88-710c-8913-d70d29cb7b4c","tenant":"planning"},"completeness":"available_evidence_only","evidence":[],"instance":{"entity":"aep.entity","fields":{"document":{"events":[],"fields":{"$aep":{"archived":false,"metadata":{"created_at":0,"id":"MIG5c4a230ee2eee1e8820b9385138e7e303da9103acc75e5d67ad0476d2ad3d586","locator":"ep://planning/store/story/pagination-cursors","provenance":{"created_by":"system","updated_by":"system"},"revision":4,"type":"aep.story/v1","updated_at":0}},"body":"## Finding\n\nThe memory backend emits `offset-<n>` cursors but ignores `after`, so following a cursor returns the first page again and durable adapters inherit the defect.\n\n## Acceptance\n\nQueries parse the existing opaque cursor, refuse malformed values with a typed error, filter and order deterministically, skip the offset, then apply the limit. A next cursor exists only when more matches remain and advances by the returned count. Entity, relation and audit pagination traverse complete result sets without duplicates; a deliberately faulty backend proves the conformance test is load-bearing.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `crates/aep-backend-memory/` query implementation and `crates/aep-contract/` errors/suites — cited.\n- durable backends through shared conformance — inferred; no separate pagination implementation expected.\n","relations":[{"derived_from":"epic:architecture-hardening"},{"serves":"vision:O2"}],"status":"implemented","summary":"Parse and apply offsets for entity, relation and audit queries.","title":"Every query cursor advances through deterministic pages"},"lifecycle_state":"implemented"}},"id":"MIG5c4a230ee2eee1e8820b9385138e7e303da9103acc75e5d67ad0476d2ad3d586","lifecycle_state":"recorded","revision":4,"version":1},"order":"per_kind_only","subject":["aep.entity","MIG5c4a230ee2eee1e8820b9385138e7e303da9103acc75e5d67ad0476d2ad3d586"]}]